{"name":"Laminated Wood","hex":"#948c7e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"laminated","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made by lamination.","Consisting of many thin layers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of laminate"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/laminated"},{"term":"wood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance making up the central part of the trunk and branches of a tree. Used as a material for construction, to manufacture various items, etc. or as fuel.","The wood from a particular species of tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or plant with trees.","To hide behind trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Engineered wood","blocks":[{"text":"Engineered wood, also called mass timber, composite wood, man-made wood, or manufactured board, includes a range of derivative wood products which are manufactured by binding or fixing the strands, particles, fibres, veneers, or boards of wood, together with adhesives, or other methods of fixation to form composite material. The panels vary in size but can range upwards of 64 by 8 feet and in the case of cross-laminated timber (CLT) can be of any thickness from a few inches to 16 inches (410 mm) or more. These products are engineered to precise design specifications, which are tested to meet national or international standards and provide uniformity and predictability in their structural performance. Engineered wood products are used in a variety of applications, from home construction to commercial buildings to industrial products. The products can be used for joists and beams that replace steel in many building projects. The term mass timber describes a group of building materials that can replace concrete assemblies. Such wood-based products typically undergo machine grading in order to be evaluated and categorized for mechanical strength and suitability for specific applications."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/EXPO-HANNOVER_8937.JPG/330px-EXPO-HANNOVER_8937.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Engineered_wood","wikidata":"Q1317763"},{"title":"Lamination","blocks":[{"text":"Lamination is the technique/process of manufacturing a material in multiple layers, so that the composite material achieves improved strength, stability, sound insulation, appearance, or other properties from the use of the differing materials, such as plastic. A laminate is a layered object or material assembled using heat, pressure, welding, or adhesives. Various coating machines, machine presses and calendering equipment are used."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Knitting_needle.ogv/330px--Knitting_needle.ogv.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamination","wikidata":"Q384943"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood","wikidata":"Q287"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Wood_plastic_composite_2.jpg/500px-Wood_plastic_composite_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Wood_plastic_composite_2.jpg/1280px-Wood_plastic_composite_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Wood_plastic_composite_2.jpg/500px-Wood_plastic_composite_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wood–plastic composite, one kind of engineered wood","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWood_plastic_composite_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/EngineeredWoodHomeDepot.jpg/500px-EngineeredWoodHomeDepot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/EngineeredWoodHomeDepot.jpg/1280px-EngineeredWoodHomeDepot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/EngineeredWoodHomeDepot.jpg/500px-EngineeredWoodHomeDepot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Engineered wood products in a Home Depot store","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEngineeredWoodHomeDepot.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/%D0%9C%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BA%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%8F%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C_%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BD_%D0%90%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B4%D1%80_%D0%94%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87.jpg/500px-%D0%9C%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BA%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%8F%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C_%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BD_%D0%90%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B4%D1%80_%D0%94%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campai